 cite32†Step 5 L43:       6. cite33†Step 6 L44:       7. cite34†Step 7 L45:     3. cite35†5.3 The Segre embedding method L46:       1. cite36†Step 1 L47:       2. cite37†Step 2 L48:       3. cite38†Step 3 L49:       4. cite39†Step 4 L50:       5. cite40†Step 5 L51:   13. cite41†References L52: cite42†License: Assumed arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L53: \nL54: arXiv:math/0606580v1 [math.NT] 23 Jun 2006\nL55: # Explicit $n$-descent on elliptic curves\nL56: I. Algebra\nL57: J.E. Cremona Address: School of Mathematical Sciences, University of Nottingham, University Park, Nottingham NG7 2RD, UK Email address: John.Cremona@nottingham.ac.uk , T.A. Fisher Address: University of Cambridge, DPMMS, Centre for Mathematical Sciences, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge CB3 0WB, UK Email address: T.A.Fisher@dpmms.cam.ac.uk , C.\nL58: O’Neil Address: Barnard College, Columbia University, Department of Mathematics, 2990 Broadway MC 4418, New York, NY 10027-6902,USA Email address: oneil@math.columbia.edu , D. Simon Address: Université de Caen, Campus II - Boulevard Maréchal Juin, BP 5186–14032, Caen, France Email address: Denis.Simon@math.unicaen.fr and M. Stoll Address: School of Engineering and Science, International University Bremen, P.O. Box 750561, 28725 Bremen, Germany Email address: M.Stoll@iu-bremen.de\nL59: Date: 1st March 2006\nL60: ###### Abstract.\nL61: \nL62: This is the first in a series of papers in which we study the $n$-Selmer group of an elliptic curve, with the aim of representing its elements as genus one normal curves of degree $n$. The methods we describe are practical in the case $n=3$ for elliptic curves over the rationals, and have been implemented in MAGMA .\nL63: ## Introduction\nL64: \nL65: Descent on an elliptic curve $E$, defined over a number field $K$, is a method for obtaining information about both the Mordell-Weil group $E(K)$ and the Tate-Shafarevich group $\\mbox{\\wncyr Sh}(E/K)$. Indeed for each integer $n\\geq 2$ there is an exact sequence\nL66: \nL67:  | $$0\\to E(K)/nE(K)\\to\\operatorname{Sel}^{(n)}(E/K)\\to\\mbox{\\wncyr Sh}(E/K)[n]\\to 0$$  |\nL68: \nL69: where $\\operatorname{Sel}^{(n)}(E/K)$ is the $n$-Selmer group.\nL70: This is the first in a series of papers in which we study the $n$-Selmer group with the aim of representing its elements as genus one normal curves $C\\subset{\\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}$ (when $n\\geq 3$). Having this representation allows searching for rational points on $C$ (which in turn gives points in $E(K)$, since $C$ may be seen as an $n$-covering of $E$) and is a first step towards doing higher descents. A further application is to the study of explicit counter-examples to the Hasse Principle.\nL71: In this introduction we discuss our approach to the problem and set out the goals of our work. Following some historical remarks, we will outline the contents of this first paper, and then briefly that of the remaining papers in the series.\nL72: The method of descent, for explicitly determining the solutions of Diophantine Equations, has a long and distinguished history going back (at least) to Fermat. As a tool for the determination of the Mordell-Weil groups of elliptic curves over number fields, descent has been used since the very first applications of computing to number theory.\nL73: Until the 1990s, the only methods which had been implemented for general elliptic curves were based on $2$-descent and could be applied only to elliptic curves defined over ${\\mathbb{Q}}$, though of course individual examples had been worked out over other fields.\nL74: The advent of higher-level computer algebra software and the development of efficient algorithms for handling the arithmetic of more general number fields has meant that $2$-descent can now be carried out over general number fields (of moderate degree and discriminant, for practical reasons). We may cite both Simon’s gp program ell.gp (see [cite43†18 ]), and the MAGMA package written primarily by Bruin, as examples of this which are widely used.\nL75: The situation regarding so-called higher descents, meaning $n$-descent for $n>2$, has until now been much more fragmentary and less satisfactory. Some $3$-descents (for twists of Fermat curves) and certain other descents via isogeny have been studied systematically, but these apply only to special families and not to general elliptic curves (at least, not without an extension of the ground field, which introduces further complexities and complicates implementation significantly).\nL76: Higher $2$-power descents (also known as second and third $2$-descents) have been studied and implemented by Siksek [cite44†10 ] and Womack [cite45†20 ] for $4$-descent, and by Stamminger [cite46†19 ] for $8$-descent.\nL77: In the case of $2$-descent, the map $C\\to{\\mathbb{P}}^{1}$ is a double cover rather than an embedding, and the elements of $\\operatorname{Sel}^{(2)}(E/K)$ are represented as curves of the form $Y^{2}=g(X)$ where $g$ is a quartic. Our goal is to be equally explicit for all $n>2$. The methods we present are fully worked out for all odd prime $n$, and have been implemented in MAGMA [cite47†9 ] in the case $n=3$ for elliptic curves over ${\\mathbb{Q}}$.\nL78: This implementation will be included in MAGMA version 2.13, to be released later this year.\nL79: To avoid making assumptions about the Galois module structure of $E[n]$, we work with the étale algebra $R$ of $E[n]$. This is a $K$-algebra of dimension $n^{2}$, explicitly realised as a product of number fields. The starting point for our work is the paper of Schaefer and Stoll [cite48†15 ], which improves on earlier methods in [cite49†6 ]. They show that if $n$ is prime then a certain group homomorphism\nL80: \nL81:  | $$w_{1}:H^{1}(K,E[n])\\to R^{\\times}/(R^{\\times})^{n}$$  |\nL82: is injective, and determine its image. This is the basis of an algorithm for computing $\\operatorname{Sel}^{(n)}(E/K)$ as a subgroup of $R^{\\times}/(R^{\\times})^{n}$. (In fact they assume that $n$ is odd, since the case $n=2$ was already well known.) The algorithm requires knowledge of the class group and unit group of each constituent field of $R$.\nL83: \nL84: In §cite20†3 we replace $w_{1}$ by a group homomorphism\nL85: \nL86:  | $$w_{2}:H^{1}(K,E[n])\\to(R\\otimes R)^{\\times}/\\partial R^{\\times}$$  |\nL87: where $\\partial:R^{\\times}\\to(R\\otimes R)^{\\times}$ is a certain map. We show that $w_{2}$ is injective for all $n\\geq 2$ and determine its image. If $n$ is prime then it is possible to convert the subgroup of $R^{\\times}/(R^{\\times})^{n}$ computed by Schaefer and Stoll to a subgroup of $(R\\otimes R)^{\\times}/\\partial R^{\\times}$. Since $R\\otimes R$ is the étale algebra of $E[n]\\times E[n]$, it is again a product of number fields.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://denisevellachemla.eu/Rosser-Schoenfeld-1962.pdf)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 31; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view0\",\"lineno\":181}); Total lines: 1796\nL108@P3: (x) li(x) > xl/2/1Og X\nL109@P3: and\nL110@P3: (x) li(x) < xl/2/log x.\nL111@P3: Indeed, Theorem 35 on p. 103 of Ingham [6] states a significantly stronger\nL112@P3: result. An analogous result for O(x) x follows from Theorem 34 on p.\nL118@P3: on p. 12 of Ingham [6], and inversion of the first gives the second, in which\nL119@P3: is the MSbius function defined on p. 567 of Landau [7], vol. 2.\nL120@P3: Once one has a good estimate for r(x) li(x), one can get an approxima\u0002tion for sums of functions of primes as follows. Using the Stieltjes integral,\nL121@P3: one has\nL122@P3: ., f(p) f(y) dr(y).\nL123@P3: p_\nL124@P3: f’(y)r(y) dy\nL125@P3: Integration by parts gives\nL126@P3: _, f(p) f(x)(x)\nL127@P3: f’(y) li(y) dy f\nL128@P3: Then integration by parts gives\nL129@P3: (2.26)\nL130@P3: _, f(p) f\nL131@P3: f(y) dy\n